ed to  find ways to be sure that the clearance  and settlement the procedures of the  broker dealers are adequate to meet  high-volume days we need to be concerned  about the order routing systems so that  they do not break down on high-volume  days we need to be concerned about  telephone access by customers to their  broker dealers and to their mutual funds  we need to be concerned most importantly  about the availability of broker capital  my tentative conclusion is that the  question of the liquidity of our markets  is one which needs to have extremely  important attention whether or not a  conclusions relating to improvements in  broker-dealer capital will require  additional legislation or regulation I  do not know but I do concern consider  that to be the crucial question for  examination  brings us to the next question which is  has the SEC found any major security  firms which lost a significant amount of  capital during or since the crash my  very able staff under the guidance of  Rick Ketchum has told me that their  inquiries in the broker-dealer area have  revealed no major firms that are in any  trouble this statistic has been  confirmed to us by the new york stock  exchange you might be interested to know  that during the during the crisis week  of October 19th our staff organized  itself into into groups and we had we  had people in direct contact with the  major brokerage firms of the United  States so that practically every hour  during these periods we were able to  confirm or check on rumors with regard  to one form or another during the period  of October 19 and 20 particular that the  street was was rampant with rumors that  one one form or another was in serious  trouble we were constantly calling those  firms calling us in New York Stock  Exchange and obtaining information and  we did not find during the market crisis  that there were major brokerage firms in  serious trouble we had as you know some  smaller firms that were not able to  handle the problems we had the true  specialists on the new york stock  exchange and two specialists on the  American Stock Exchange who were not  able to survive they were acquired by  other companies and their specialist  functions continued  this questioner says why should the SEC  be concerned about the  internationalization of the securities  market why not let those international  markets work out their own problems if  the fact is that internationalization is  here whether we like it or not there is  an enormous amount of activity taking  place in the overseas markets it began  in the dead area in which American firms  went overseas to borrow it is continuing  in the equity area since the so-called  Big Bang of last spring in in London in  which the of the city the acronym for  for Wall Street in London was  deregulated there has emerged in in  London and in it in a very large market  for securities that market is is trading  in in international issues as well as  trading in some of our major American  issues at the same time we've had the  emergence in Japan of an extremely large  and well capitalized market there are in  Japan only four major broker-dealer  firms these firms are extremely well  capitalized and that market proceeds  with the depth and liquidity which is  very strong what is what is happening in  in the international markets is that  some of the demand for securities  transactions which exists in our in the  United States is going overseas and at  the same time our markets are not able  at this point to to deal with the you  know the problem of attracting overseas  securities to to our to the United  States we have disclosure restrictions  which are important barriers  they're bringing these securities to our  markets one of the things that I talked  about in in my remarks was the  possibility that we may be able to find  a way to bring these securities to  market here even in a restricted  institutionalized market but  nevertheless to provide a vehicle for  that training the other the other point  which is extremely important to  understand in the international  internationalisation area is that  automation is here to stay we have now  in this in the United States developed  automated quotation systems last Christ  reporting systems routing systems  clearance and settlement systems and in  some case X cases execution systems all  of these systems are taking place in the  United States in an automated way and  what's occurring now is that these  automation systems are being developed  overseas with links between our markets  so that inevitably what's going to  happen in the future is that as private  enterprises begin to develop automation  of securities systems we're going to see  our security sold in international  markets a greater depth and liquidity  with our with our securities and  international markets and we are going  to be faced with both regulatory and  competitive problems in the  international area in the futures market  area in the futures trading area it's  clear that that the futures trading a  derivative index products trading will  exist in overseas markets and that if we  tamper I think that if we tamper too  much with our own markets we're simply  going to see that the activity move  overseas with the possibility again of  having an unregulated markets over there  my view is that the Securities and  Exchange Commission needs to cooperate  with regulators overseas we need to  convince the regulators in foreign  markets that one of the reasons that our  markets are so good so strong so say  is that we have a regulation I'm pleased  to tell you that that in the insider  trading area we have found countries  more willing to adopt insider trading  legislation to put their regulation more  in line with ours so that we have the  equivalent of equal regulation oversee  so that's a long answer the short answer  is at these two markets our markets and  international markets are inexorably  linked d